      {
        "number": 9,
        "internalPageId": null,
        "title": "9",
        "visibleTexts": [
          "9",
          "TO CONSIDER 1. To talk about relationships among family members, we normally use the possessive case (‘s) which denotes belonging and possession and in this case states family relationships. 2. There is more than one scenario for the possessive case: a. Joe’s wife is a doctor. - One owner and one possession. b. Those are my aunt’s (kids). - When the noun was already mentioned and we do not need to state again. c. This is Ted and Mary’s child. - Two owners - One possession. d. These are Jake’s and Jill’s children - Two owners and two possessions. 3. When the noun finishes in ‘s’ we only add the (‘) and the end of the word. E.G. This is James’ child. When this happens, we add an extra syllable in spoken language that is pronounced /әz/. 4. This is not only used to talk about family. E.G. Adrian’s car is blue. 5. When we talk about objects or animals, it is common to use: The legs of the table / The tail of the cat. However, you may find examples using the (‘s)."
        ],
        "links": [],
